    "case_id": "241",
    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old man with a 12-year history of perianal fistula presented with recent mucinous discharge, pain, and perianal induration. One year earlier, he had undergone incision and drainage of a recurrent perianal abscess. \n\nOn examination, there were two indurated, ulcerative lesions each 7 cm in diameter and three external fistula openings around the anus; there was no inguinal lymphadenopathy.\n\nUpper endoscopy was unremarkable. Colonoscopy showed the internal fistula opening at the right side of the dentate line, with otherwise normal mucosa in the rectum and colon on digital and endoscopic examination.\n\nAbdominopelvic CT and MRI demonstrated a well-defined mass at the level of the anorectal junction extending into the right ischiorectal fossa, with enlarged pelvic lymph nodes but no inguinal nodal or distant metastases.\n\nHistological examination of a biopsy specimen from the indurated area revealed mucinous adenocarcinoma. A serum carcinoembryonic antigen level was within the normal range.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Perianal mucinous adenocarcinoma\nB. Rectal primary carcinoma with direct extension was considered but excluded because “no evidence of mucosal lesion in\nC. Secondary deposition of malignant cells from a proximal gastrointestinal carcinoma was considered, prompting full GI\nD. Anal gland adenocarcinoma was an alternative origin, as “many authors have favored an origin in the anal glands\nE. A carcinoma arising de novo in a long\nF. Benign inflammatory or infectious perianal disease was a mimic, as “symptoms often initially mimic benign inflammatory\nG. None\nH. None\n",
